# onlineornot_token (Resource)

Manages an API token. Configuration changes replace the token. The secret is only returned on creation and is stored in Terraform state.

The provider credential needs `API_TOKENS` `EDIT` for creation/deletion (which implies `READ` for refresh), plus delegation authority for every requested grant. `EDIT` permits delegating `READ` or `EDIT` for the same scope; `READ` cannot delegate `EDIT`. Use a separate management credential, not the token being managed.

## Log sending permissions

`LOGS:SEND` grants sending access only, not log-read or management access. It can be combined with other explicitly requested grants. `LOGS:EDIT` permits delegating `LOGS:SEND` but does not itself grant sending access.

Set `project_id` to the project receiving logs, or omit it/use null for unassigned. A project ID requires a `LOGS:SEND` grant. Project scope applies only to sending; other grants remain organisation-wide. Changing or removing the project ID replaces the token rather than moving its sending destination.

A provider credential relying on `LOGS:SEND` may delegate sending only to its own project, including unassigned. Organisation-wide `LOGS:EDIT` permits issuing sending credentials for other projects. Assigned tokens must be revoked before deleting their project.

## Expiration and replacement

Omit `expires_at` and leave `never_expires = false` to use the API default of **365 days from creation**. `expires_after` records the actual date, so refresh does not recalculate this default. Set `expires_at` for a specific UTC timestamp, or explicitly set `never_expires = true` for no expiration. Do not combine those settings.

The API has no update endpoint. Editing the name, grants, sending project, or expiration settings destroys and recreates the token. Replacement issues a new secret and revokes the old token. Consider `create_before_destroy` and coordinate consumers to avoid interruption; Terraform cannot automatically update external consumers. Revoked or expired provider credentials may prevent refresh or destruction.

## Secret handling

`token` is sensitive and computed, returned only on creation, and retained during refresh. **Sensitive does not encrypt state**: protect Terraform state, saved plans, backups, and outputs with access controls and encryption. Do not commit them or enable verbose logs around credentials.

Import retrieves metadata only: the secret remains null and cannot be recovered. Keep the existing secret separately or deliberately replace the resource to issue a new one. Deleting an already-absent token succeeds; a read returning HTTP 404 removes it from state. Authentication and other API errors do not remove state.

